) in a Go package with an existing single-call test fixture: (1) `DUAL-FIXTURE REUSE — EXISTING SINGLE-CALL FIXTURE FOR THE COVERAGE MEMBER, A FILE-LOCAL CONCURRENT FIXTURE FOR THE BURST MEMBER` — the canonical pair lives in `package audit_test` (the same external test package as the existing `audit_test.go`) so the coverage member reuses the existing `fakeRecorder` / `newAuditor` / `ctxWith` / `orgID` helpers verbatim. The contention burst introduces a SECOND in-file recorder, `concurrentAuditRecorder`, with a mutex-guarded events slice and a `snapshot()` accessor — declaring it in the new test file (not in `audit_test.go`) keeps the existing `fakeRecorder`'s zero-mutex shape for the single-call unit tests that rely on it. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose contention burst would race the existing single-call fixture: don't retrofit a mutex onto the existing helper — add a sibling helper in the new test file. The BE-0395 pattern called for "extend, don't fork" the existing fixtures; BE-0396 refines that to "extend the coverage helper, add a sibling for the contention burst" when the existing helper's zero-mutex shape is part of its contract. (2) `CLOSED-SET SCENARIO TABLE WALKS ALLOWED *AND* DENIED VARIANTS PER ACTION SURFACE` — the audit-completeness contract is not "every recorded event is well-formed" — it's "every security-relevant decision IS recorded AND well-formed", and denied decisions are as load-bearing as allowed ones (an audit log that omits denials is incomplete by definition). The scenario table therefore enumerates the canonical policy.Action surface (organization.create allowed, project.create allowed, environment.create allowed, service.create allowed, project.delete denied cross-tenant, environment.delete denied out-of-scope, keys.manage denied no-capability, limits.write denied principal-disabled) and the per-scenario `wantDecision` field pins the recorded `store.AuditDecision` to `AuditDecisionAllowed` or `AuditDecisionDenied` so a regression that flipped the recorder's verdict literal trips per-scenario. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose subject is a chokepoint that records BOTH outcomes (audit / metrics counter / structured log emitter / outbox): the closed-set scenario table MUST cover both verdicts, not just the happy path. (3) `PER-EMITTER FIELD-FIDELITY UNDER CONTENTION VIA EXPECTED-SET / OBSERVED-SET DIFF` — the runtime burst seeds `auditCompletenessWorkers * auditCompletenessIterationsPerWorker = 32` concurrent goroutines, each emitting a recorded decision with a unique `req_audit_completeness_burst_<w>_<i>` request_id. After the burst, the test builds two maps (expected: the closed set of synthesized request_ids; observed: the set of request_ids on captured events) and reports both missing-in-observed (a dropped emission OR a cross-write that overwrote one emitter's RequestID with another's — both surface here) AND unexpected-in-observed (a spurious event OR a cross-write that produced a new request_id under contention). Asserting on the SET (not just the count) is what catches the cross-write regression — a recorder that swapped two emitters' RequestIDs under a race would pass the count assertion but fail the set diff. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose contention burst's contract is "every emitter's identifying field is preserved on its recorded event": build expected/observed sets keyed on the emitter-unique identifier, then diff both ways. The burst's auditWorkers=4 × auditIters=8 sizing matches the BE-0395 burst sizing (32 concurrent calls is the smallest power-of-two-burst-table-pair where a non-thread-safe recorder fails under -race ~99% of the time, while keeping the wall-clock under 1s on CI).
